---
import StoryLayout from "../../layouts/StoryLayout.astro";
import StoryChapter from "../../components/StoryChapter.astro";
import CaseJourney from "../../components/CaseJourney.astro";
import Clip from "../../components/Clip.astro";
import ClipSet from "../../components/ClipSet.astro";
import ArchitectureCompare from "../../components/ArchitectureCompare.astro";

const steps = [
  {
    key: "find",
    label: "Find your night",
    text: [
      "Search by genre, area or time and the list narrows as you type. The map splits Paris into its 20 arrondissements and clusters the events, but you never have to wait for it: if the map tiles fail to load, search and the list carry on.",
    ],
    caption: "Searching “amapiano” in the 2026 archive, then opening the arrondissement map.",
  },
  {
    key: "plan",
    label: "Plan it",
    text: [
      "Choose how many stops, the vibe and a budget, and it suggests a route. It favours events you've saved, orders the stops by start time and penalises long walks between them.",
      "Then the route goes to your calendar, or opens as walking directions in Google or Apple Maps.",
    ],
    caption: "Asking for a four-stop party route, then scrolling through the suggestion.",
  },
  {
    key: "offline",
    label: "Keep it with no signal",
    text: [
      "The night happens on a crowded street, on a phone with one bar. Fête Finder keeps a copy of the programme on the device, so search still works offline. Save an event without signal and the change waits in a queue, then goes through once you're back online.",
    ],
    caption: "Recorded with the network switched off: the cached-programme notice, a search, then saving an event.",
  },
];

const chapters = [
  { id: "problem", label: "The problem" },
  { id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works" },
  { id: "what-happened", label: "What happened" },
  {
    id: "under-the-hood",
    label: "Under the hood",
    children: [
      { id: "sheet-to-postgres", label: "From a Sheet to a database" },
      { id: "agents", label: "Agents that find the events" },
      { id: "flyers", label: "Flyers into listings" },
      { id: "stable-links", label: "Links that survive edits" },
      { id: "offline-properly", label: "Offline, properly" },
      { id: "exchange", label: "Money and the exchange" },
      { id: "architecture", label: "Architecture" },
    ],
  },
  { id: "lessons", label: "What I learned" },
];
---

<StoryLayout
  title="Fête Finder"
  description="Search, route planning and offline support for Fête de la Musique in Paris, with the June 2026 numbers and how it was built."
  chapters={chapters}
  groups={[{ id: "night", threads: steps }]}
>
  <StoryChapter id="problem" title="The problem">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Fête de la Musique puts music on the streets of Paris every June. The 2026 programme Fête Finder carried had 159 events, spread from Friday 19 to Monday 22 June.</p>
      <p>Hardly anyone arrives knowing an event's name. They know the part of town they'll be in, the music they like, and whether they want something free or something late. Then they need a plan their friends can follow.</p>
      <p>And the whole thing happens outside, on phones, with bad signal.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="how-it-works" title="How it works" group="night">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Three steps, recorded on the live site. It's the 2026 archive now, so the events are over, but search, plans and offline mode still work.</p>
    </div>
    <CaseJourney project="fete-finder" steps={steps} />
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="what-happened" title="What happened">
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>The first version went up in June 2025, built in under five days from the team's Google Sheet of events. Out of Office Collective reported over 1,000 registered users in its first 48 hours, 13,000 unique visits and 20,000 page views, and the listings grew from 35 to over 80 that week as the community sent them in.</p>
    </div>
    <blockquote class="pull-quote">
      <p>“We're actively adding new features and filters based on real-time feedback from people actually using the tool. It's a co-created solution for a shared problem.”</p>
      <footer>Mel, co-founder of Out of Office Collective, on LinkedIn, June 2025</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>The 2026 season ran on the rebuilt platform, and this time I counted it myself.</p>
    </div>
    <table class="story-figures">
      <caption>Fête Finder, 2026 season. Counted from a production backup taken on 3 July.</caption>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Unique visitors in June</th><td>about 10,900</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Unique visitors over the festival weekend, 19–22 June</th><td>4,221</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">People signed up, February to June</th><td>2,601</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Events listed</th><td>159</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Page views in June</th><td>54,295</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Outbound clicks</th><td>10,270</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Save toggles (saves and unsaves)</th><td>2,855</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Map opens</th><td>914</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Tracked actions on 21 June alone</th><td>26,378</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Unique visitors are counted the way analytics tools count them: one per device, so someone on a phone and a laptop counts twice. Sign-ups are accounts. The rest are counts of actions, not people, ticket sales or attendance.</p>
      <p>The ticket exchange also travelled beyond the site, into a WhatsApp community of about 2,000 people. More on the bot below.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="under-the-hood" title="Under the hood" part>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>For engineers: the parts I enjoyed building, and the decisions behind them.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="sheet-to-postgres" title="From a Sheet to a database" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>In 2025 the programme lived in a Google Sheet. The team typed listings into it by hand, and the site read it live through a service account, with caches and a CSV copy in front so a slow or failed read couldn't take the site down. Keeping those caches in step with the Sheet took several fixes that June, and the Sheet kept no record of who changed what.</p>
      <p>For 2026 I moved the programme into Postgres, edited through an event sheet inside the admin. It looks like the spreadsheet the team already knew, but every save is a revision: between May and the festival it recorded 134 of them, 95 autosaves and 39 publishes, so any change can be traced, and the store keeps backups to restore from.</p>
      <p>That gave the agents and the flyer tool somewhere safe to put their work: draft rows that nothing public reads until a person accepts them.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="agents" title="Agents that find the events" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Before this, Mel on the team collected screenshots all day and bulk-uploaded them each evening, about 30 to 40 hours of work a season.</p>
      <p>So I built a team of agents that ran on a VPS, each driving a browser. Ticketing sites like Resident Advisor and Fatsoma were straightforward. Organisers' social pages blocked direct collection, so the agents worked through accounts and page-monitoring services that flagged new posts.</p>
      <p>For each possible event, an agent read the organiser's profile, the post and the replies, ran OCR on the flyer, and had Gemini fill in the listing: names and descriptions as free text, and price, age range and indoor or outdoor as fixed choices. Every field carried a confidence score.</p>
      <p>Google's location API turned venues into coordinates, and known venues came from a cache, so the same bar was never looked up twice.</p>
      <p>Anything doubtful went to a person: a missing date, a possible duplicate, a place Google couldn't find. Drafts landed in the same review queue as the flyer tool below, and nothing went public until someone accepted it.</p>
      <p>In the two weeks before the 2026 festival the agents suggested 87 of the 159 listings. All were used, about 20 needed a correction, mostly a clearer location, and none were rejected.</p>
      <p>Agents collect and draft; people publish.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="flyers" title="Flyers into listings" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Organisers also send flyers and screenshots straight to the team. An admin can drop in up to six images of one event and Gemini drafts the listing from them, into the same review queue the agents use.</p>
      <p>Every field comes back with its value, the evidence it read, a confidence score and up to three alternatives. The prompt treats text inside an image as evidence, never as instructions, which matters when anyone can design a flyer.</p>
      <p>The draft opens in a review screen with that evidence beside each field. A person edits it and accepts it. Until then the row is marked as a draft, and drafts are filtered out of everything public.</p>
      <p>One honest gap: nothing yet checks whether those confidence scores are calibrated. That's the next measurement I'd add.</p>
    </div>
    <ClipSet
      project="fete-finder"
      clips={[
        { beat: "flyer-draft", caption: "An admin drops in a flyer and gets a draft listing.", local: true },
        { beat: "flyer-review", caption: "Each field shows the evidence it was read from and a confidence score; the admin picks what to keep.", local: true },
        { beat: "flyer-sheet", caption: "The accepted draft becomes a new revision in the sheet, visible only to admins until it's published.", local: true },
      ]}
    />
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="stable-links" title="Links that survive edits" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Event details change constantly in the week before. Each event gets a key built from a hash, like <code>evt_</code> plus a short digest, and keeps it for life once it has one. Series get their own <code>ser_</code> keys.</p>
      <p>The URL is that key plus an optional slug. Rename the event and the slug can change, but the link someone posted in a group chat on Tuesday still opens the right event on Saturday.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="offline-properly" title="Offline, properly" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>The service worker caches public pages and event details, and nothing else. Admin, sign-in, user and webhook routes are never cached, so nothing private ends up stored on the phone.</p>
      <p>The programme snapshot lives in IndexedDB with a schema name and version. If the app changes shape, an old snapshot is thrown away instead of being misread.</p>
      <p>Changes made offline, like saves and route edits, go into a queue. Each one carries an idempotency key and belongs to the person who made it, and repeats collapse. Tapping save three times in a tunnel sends one change.</p>
      <p>The map is an extra, not a dependency: if its tiles fail to load, search, filters and the list keep working.</p>
    </div>
  </StoryChapter>

  <StoryChapter id="exchange" title="Money and the exchange" level={3}>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>Partners could pay through Stripe Checkout to promote an event. The webhook checks Stripe's signature and stores each event once under its Stripe event ID, so a retried delivery can't activate a placement twice.</p>
      <p>The ticket exchange is where people post tickets they're selling or looking for. Anyone can report a listing as a scam, the wrong event, a misleading price, abusive contact or spam, and admins see a count of reports waiting for review.</p>
    </div>
    <Clip project="fete-finder" beat="exchange" caption="The ticket exchange, with listings grouped by event. Nothing is posted or replied to." />
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>The WhatsApp bot is a separate small service. It asks the site for new listings using a shared secret, posts each one to the community chat with a link back, then confirms the post so nothing goes out twice.</p>
      <p>It moderates the chat too, with link and spam rules and strikes that expire after seven days. It starts in dry-run mode by default, which is the right default for a bot that can remove people.</p>
    </div>
    <ArchitectureCompare
      id="arch-fete"
      before={{
        title: "How Fête Finder's listings worked in 2025",
        summary:
          "2025: Mel on the team screenshotted organisers' posts and flyers all day and typed them into a Google Sheet by hand, about 30 to 40 hours of work a season. The site read the Sheet live through a service account, with caches and a CSV fallback in front of it that kept falling out of sync. The Sheet had no history of who changed what.",
      }}
      after={{
        title: "How Fête Finder's listings worked in 2026",
        summary:
          "2026: agents on a VPS watch ticketing sites and organisers' pages, read each possible event with OCR and Gemini, and place venues through Google's location API with a cache. Flyers sent to the team join at the Gemini step. Every draft goes to human review before it reaches Postgres, which replaced the Google Sheet and keeps a revision for every change, and then the public site. New ticket-exchange listings go to the community chat through the WhatsApp bot.",
      }}
      width={800}
      height={336}
      boxes={[
        { id: "posts", label: "Organisers' posts", note: "flyers · screenshots", x: 20, y: 30, w: 200 },
        { id: "manual", label: "Mel, by hand", weak: "screenshots all day", in: "before", x: 250, y: 30, w: 200 },
        { id: "sheet", label: "Google Sheet", weak: "hand edits, no history", in: "before", x: 480, y: 30, w: 200 },
        { id: "cache", label: "Caches + CSV", weak: "fell out of sync", in: "before", x: 480, y: 140, w: 200 },
        { id: "agents", label: "Agents on a VPS", note: "RA · Fatsoma · organiser pages", fix: "87 of 159 listings", in: "after", x: 250, y: 30, w: 210, h: 70 },
        { id: "places", label: "Google location", note: "venue cache", in: "after", x: 480, y: 30, w: 200 },
        { id: "flyers", label: "Flyer images", note: "up to six", in: "after", x: 20, y: 140, w: 150 },
        { id: "gemini", label: "Gemini draft", note: "evidence · confidence", in: "after", x: 190, y: 140, w: 170 },
        { id: "review", label: "Human review", in: "after", x: 380, y: 140, w: 130 },
        { id: "postgres", label: "Postgres", note: "events · revisions", fix: "replaced the Sheet", in: "after", x: 530, y: 140, w: 180, h: 70 },
        { id: "site", label: "Site", note: "public", x: 724, y: 140, w: 60 },
        { id: "exchange", label: "Ticket exchange", note: "new listings", in: "after", x: 20, y: 256, w: 200 },
        { id: "bot", label: "WhatsApp bot", note: "posts once, links back", in: "after", x: 260, y: 256, w: 220 },
        { id: "chat", label: "Community chat", note: "about 2,000 people", in: "after", x: 520, y: 256, w: 260 },
      ]}
      notes={[{ text: "about 30–40 hours a season", tone: "weak", x: 250, y: 112 }]}
      edges={[
        { from: "posts", to: "manual", in: "before" },
        { from: "manual", to: "sheet", in: "before" },
